 ☕️ Good Monday afternoon. Today's newsletter, edited by Alex Fitzpatrick, is 654 words, a 2½-min. read. Thanks to Sheryl Miller for copy editing . 

 ⚡️ Situational awareness: President Trump lashed out on Truth Social at calls for new AI guardrails and protections, arguing AI only needs a "STRONG AND SMART (High IQ!) PRESIDENT" and that AI critics should "BEWARE!" Go deeper . 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Anthropic IPO still on schedule 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios Anthropic is still likely to go public this year, even as AI safety rockets into the zeitgeist, multiple sources tell Axios' Dan Primack. 

 There have been whispers that Anthropic might want to wait, due to new product liability and regulation questions.

 Yet the Claude maker might actually view the focus on AI risks as elevating the case for its IPO rather than dampening it, arguing that safety is improved via the transparency of being a public company. OpenAI, meanwhile, was leaning toward a 2027 listing — and still seems to be doing so. 🖼️ The big picture: Anthropic's business, and investor FOMO, are unchanged from last week.

 Yes, Anthropic has pledged to have third-party evaluation of its models, and suggested some pacing that might slow new model releases. But so did its top rival — and there's still an insane amount of untapped revenue tied to existing models. 🤖 It's also not like Anthropic would be the first or only listed frontier AI lab, as Alphabet and SpaceX shares continue to trade.

 There's also a cynical argument that "pacing" could reduce Anthropic's compute spend, thus improving its financials. Some accuse the company of pursuing a policy of regulatory capture — and if you buy that argument, you'll probably also buy Anthropic stock.

 Finally, it still needs the money. It could delay and raise more via private markets, but no frontier lab can just sit on the capital markets sidelines. Go deeper . 

 
 
